  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The test is general acceptability, not the material or the issuer.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Barter is exchange without the intermediary of money.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Each party must want what the other has, at the same time.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Money stands between sale and purchase, so the two no longer coincide.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Pricing goods in one unit is the measure-of-value function.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Keeping purchasing power between dates is the store-of-value function.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A debt fixed in money and settled later needs money as a standard of deferred payment.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A deposit is money in the economist's sense, but a creditor need not accept a transfer of it.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Legal tender is the narrow set a creditor is obliged by law to accept.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A cheque instructs the bank; it is not itself money and may be refused.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Portability is the ease of carrying value from place to place.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A cow cannot be split to pay for a loaf of bread.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Yams rot, so they cannot store value.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Homogeneity removes any argument about which unit is being paid.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Stability of value is what makes saving and lending possible.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A treasury bill or fixed deposit is near money.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Cowries had value in themselves as well as in exchange.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Fiat money rests on law and public confidence, not on metal.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Deposits are bank money, and they are the largest part of the modern supply.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Electronic money is stored value spent by an electronic transfer.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    M1 counts what can be spent at once: cash and current accounts.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    M2 adds the near money held in savings and fixed deposits.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Prices rise faster than the note can be spent, so its value melts.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Without a common measure every bargain is a fresh argument about rates.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Whatever a community generally accepts is money there.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Money does not produce; it facilitates exchange.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Perishability is the opposite of a quality money must have.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    It is not spendable at once, but it turns into money quickly and at little loss.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The card borrows money for you; it is not itself a store of value.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Acceptability, durability and small denominations made them workable.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Cash is spendable at once and loses nothing when it is spent.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Those three qualities made it acceptable, divisible and stable in value.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Convertibility was the gold-standard promise.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Deposits created by bank lending dwarf the note issue.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Commodity money is valued for itself as well as for exchange.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The debt is written in money, so both parties know what will be paid.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Legal tender is a subset of money, so every legal tender note is money; the reverse is false.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Each note buys less, so its purchasing power falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The fourth function fixes debts for later settlement.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Two people must each want exactly what the other has, at the same moment.
  ]

]

